/*!
  \page pedigree_whatsdifferent What's Different in Pedigree?

Pedigree differs from traditional UNIX systems in a few important ways. This
page describes these and also clarifies why.

\section Filesystem

Pedigree uses a single rooted namespace. A path beginning with `/` is always
absolute and begins at the root filesystem. Programs, libraries, configuration,
and user data use the conventional FHS locations under `/usr`, `/etc`, `/var`,
and `/home`.

Additional filesystems receive stable mount names beneath `/media`. For
example, raw disk access is available at `/media/raw` and the temporary RAM
filesystem is available at `/media/scratch`. System interfaces are also exposed
at their conventional locations such as `/dev`, `/proc`, `/run`, and `/tmp`.

\subsection posix_compat POSIX Path Compatibility

POSIX paths are native VFS paths. No path prefix or mount separator is needed.

*/
